From e7624ec228628e1f8edbb087827ec3ce411f9c5e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vincenzo Grimaldi <vince.ceccarelli@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 12:41:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0050 fly launch rewrote the config it had just been checked
 against (5888 passed)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Your verify failed, and it failed for the right reason. `git status` showed
`M bot/fly.toml`: fly launch had rewritten it on the run that first
deployed, printing "Wrote config file fly.toml", so the check in step 2 of
deploy.sh had validated a document that no longer existed by step 7. The
test added in 0042 — which parses the COMMITTED fly.toml and runs
fly_stack.check() on it — then went red. A true positive.

What launch adds is exactly what this book cannot have: it reads the
Dockerfile's EXPOSE and offers an [http_service], which drags in auto_stop,
and a machine stopped while holding a hedge leaves a client unhedged.

`fly apps create` registers the app and writes nothing. fly.toml stays the
file that was checked and the file that is deployed. A test refuses any
launch INVOCATION while still allowing the word in a comment or an echo,
because a mention is not a call.

D38 — and reading this repo's own product law properly caught a second one.
The drill honoured STATE_DB_PATH, which on Fly names the RUNNING BOOK's
SQLite. The drill is a separate process, so that is two writers on one file:
against the one-writer rule, and against a ledger that is supposed to
explain the entire equity change. It also read AND WROTE os.environ to steer
persistence, when config.load() is the only environment reader here — a tool
that mutates the environment to redirect another module is a second reader
wearing a disguise.

The drill now constructs its own scratch StateStore and passes it to
build_bot. It needs nothing from the book's database: its own journal, and
its position read from the VENUE. An AST test refuses any os.environ or
os.getenv in the module.

